---
import GuideLayout from "@/layouts/GuideLayout.astro";
import { GUIDES_BY_SLUG } from "@/data/guides";
import { businessDaysInYear } from "@tinytools/calc";
const g = GUIDES_BY_SLUG["how-many-work-hours-in-a-year"]!;

const years = [2025, 2026, 2027, 2028].map((y) => ({
  y,
  days: businessDaysInYear(y),
  hours: businessDaysInYear(y) * 8,
}));
---

<GuideLayout title={g.title} metaTitle={g.metaTitle} description={g.description} slug={g.slug} updated={g.updated}>
  <p>
    A standard full-time work year is <strong>2,080 hours</strong>: 40 hours a
    week × 52 weeks. That is the figure used to convert salaries to hourly rates
    and to budget headcount. But the hours you <em>actually</em> work are usually
    fewer, and the number of working days shifts from year to year.
  </p>

  <h2>The standard: 2,080 hours</h2>
  <p><code>40 hours/week × 52 weeks = 2,080 hours</code></p>
  <p>
    Divide an annual salary by 2,080 for the equivalent hourly rate: $60,000 ÷
    2,080 ≈ $28.85. Use the
    <a href="/calculators/salary-to-hourly-calculator/">Salary to Hourly
    Calculator</a> for other schedules.
  </p>

  <h2>Working days by year</h2>
  <p>
    Count only Monday–Friday dates and the total moves between roughly 260 and
    262, depending on how weekends fall and whether it is a leap year. Public
    holidays (which vary by country) reduce it further.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Year</th><th>Weekdays (Mon–Fri)</th><th>Hours at 8/day</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {years.map((r) => (<tr><td>{r.y}</td><td>{r.days}</td><td>{r.hours.toLocaleString()}</td></tr>))}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    These exclude public holidays. Subtract your country's holidays — typically
    8–13 days — for actual working days.
  </p>

  <h2>Hours you actually work</h2>
  <p><code>hours worked = 2,080 − (PTO days + holidays) × hours per workday</code></p>
  <p>
    With 15 vacation days and 10 public holidays at 8 hours each, that is 2,080 −
    200 = <strong>1,880 hours</strong>. Part-time and compressed schedules change
    the starting figure.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Hours/week</th><th>Per year (52 wk)</th><th>Per month</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>40</td><td>2,080</td><td>173.3</td></tr>
      <tr><td>37.5</td><td>1,950</td><td>162.5</td></tr>
      <tr><td>35</td><td>1,820</td><td>151.7</td></tr>
      <tr><td>30</td><td>1,560</td><td>130.0</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Why it matters</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Salary comparisons:</strong> a job expecting 45-hour weeks has a lower real hourly rate than one at 40.</li>
    <li><strong>Contractor rates:</strong> contractors have no paid time off, so they need a higher rate to match an equivalent salary.</li>
    <li><strong>Project planning:</strong> a full-time employee provides roughly 1,850–1,900 productive hours a year, not 2,080.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Calculate it for your schedule</h2>
  <p>
    The <a href="/calculators/working-hours-calculator/">Working Hours
    Calculator</a> takes your hours per week, weeks per year, PTO, and holidays
    and returns hours per week, month, and year, plus working days and full-time
    equivalent.
  </p>
</GuideLayout>
